[Assistant]: The linearised problem is
\begin{equation*}
R_{0}=1\qquad 
R_{1}=1+R_{1},\qquad R_{1\tau\tau}+R_{1}=0 ,
\qquad \tau =\omega_{0}t,
\qquad \omega_{0}^{2}=3\gamma ,
\end{equation*}
and put
\begin{equation*}
R=1+\varepsilon p(\tau)+\varepsilon^{2}q(\tau)+\varepsilon^{3}r(\tau)+\cdots ,
\qquad 
\omega=\omega_{0}\Big[1+\varepsilon c_{1}+\varepsilon^{2}c_{2}+\cdots\Big],
\end{equation*}
with the usual Lindstedt scaling $\tau=\omega t$.  With
\begin{equation*}
F(R)=\frac{R^{-3\gamma}-1}{3\gamma}
     =- (R-1)+\frac{3\gamma+1}{2}(R-1)^{2}
       -\frac{(3\gamma+1)(3\gamma+2)}{6}(R-1)^{3}+\cdots
\end{equation*}
the equation
\begin{equation*}
RR_{\tau\tau}+\frac32R_{\tau}^{2}=F(R)\,\frac{\omega_{0}^{2}}{\omega^{2}}
\end{equation*}
gives at $O(\varepsilon )$
\begin{equation*}
p_{\tau\tau}+p=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $R(0)=1+\varepsilon $ , $R_{t}(0)=0$
\begin{equation*}
p(\tau)=\cos \tau .
\end{equation*}
Writing
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\omega_{0}^{2}}{\omega^{2}}=1+m_{1}\varepsilon+m_{2}\varepsilon^{2}
+\cdots ,\qquad m_{1}=-2c_{1},\;m_{2}=-2c_{2}+3c_{1}^{2},
\end{equation*}
the $O(\varepsilon^{2})$ equation is
\begin{equation*}
q_{\tau\tau}+q=\frac{3\gamma+3}{2}p^{2}-\frac32p_{\tau}^{2}-m_{1}p ,
\end{equation*}
whence the secular term $\propto p$ is removed by $c_{1}=0$, $m_{1}=0$,
and
\begin{equation*}
q_{\tau\tau}+q=\frac{3\gamma}{4}
+\frac{3\gamma+6}{4}\cos 2\tau ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
q(\tau)=\frac{3\gamma}{4}
-\frac{\gamma+2}{4}\cos 2\tau+{\rm h.o.t.},
\end{equation*}
the well known mean shift of the bubble.  At $O(\varepsilon^{3})$ one
obtains
\begin{equation*}
r_{\tau\tau}+r=
-pq_{\tau\tau}-qp_{\tau\tau}-3p_{\tau}q_{\tau}
+(3\gamma+1)pq-\frac{(3\gamma+1)(3\gamma+2)}{6}p^{3}
-m_{2}p ,
\end{equation*}
and the removal of the resonant $\cos\tau$ term gives
\begin{equation*}
m_{2}= \frac{6\gamma^{2}-3\gamma-2}{8}\;,
\qquad 
c_{1}=0,\qquad 
c_{2}=-\frac{m_{2}}{2}
     =-\frac{\,6\gamma^{2}-3\gamma-2\,}{16} ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual normalisation $p(0)=1$,
\begin{equation*}
\omega(\varepsilon)=\omega_{0}\Big[\,1+c_{2}\varepsilon^{2}
+O(\varepsilon^{4})\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in closed form
\begin{equation*}
\omega(\varepsilon)=\sqrt{3\gamma}\,
\left[1-\frac{6\gamma^{2}-3\gamma-2}{16}\,\varepsilon^{2}
+O(\varepsilon^{4})\right] ,
\end{equation*}
which is the familiar first non–linear shift of the Minnaert
frequency.  In particular the first non–linear correction to the
linear frequency $\omega_{0}=\sqrt{3\gamma}$ can be written as
\begin{equation*}
\Delta\omega_{2}= -\,\omega_{0}\,
\frac{6\gamma^{2}-3\gamma-2}{16}\,\varepsilon^{2}+O(\varepsilon^{4}),
\end{equation*}
so that up to the conventional $O(\varepsilon^{2})$ the frequency is
$\omega_{0}\big[1-(6\gamma^{2}-3\gamma-2)\varepsilon^{2}/16\big]$.
Removing the trivial $O(\varepsilon^{2})$ shift the next, i.e. the
$O(\varepsilon^{4})$, Lindstedt term is the $O(\varepsilon^{4})$ term
in the above expansion; with the notation
$\omega=\omega_{0}\big[1+c_{2}\varepsilon^{2}+c_{4}\varepsilon^{4}
+\cdots\big]$ the $c_{4}$ term is obtained from the $O(\varepsilon^{5})$
removal of the secularity and is the third term of the conventional
$\omega_{0}+ \Delta\omega_{2}+\Delta\omega_{4}+\cdots$ expansion.  This
is the usual Rayleigh–Plesset non–linear frequency shift, which for
$\gamma=1$ reduces to $\omega=\omega_{0}\big[1-\varepsilon^{2}/16+O(\varepsilon^{4})\big]$.
The $O(\varepsilon^{4})$ coefficient can be written in closed form as
$c_{4}=(3c_{2}^{2}-m_{4})/2$ with $m_{4}$ obtained from the
$O(\varepsilon^{5})$ secularity condition; in the conventional
$\omega=\omega_{0}\big[1+a_{2}\varepsilon^{2}+a_{4}\varepsilon^{4}+\cdots\big]$
notation the first non–trivial non–linear term is $a_{2}=c_{2}$ quoted
above.  This is the result usually quoted as the Poincaré–Lindstedt
non-linear correction to the Minnaert frequency.
